<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Everard t'Serclaes</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1320–<a href="/years/1388">1388</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Everard t'Serclaes was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a> patrician and a hero of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-brabantine-succession">War of the Brabantine Succession</a>. In October 1356, after the Flemish had occupied Brussels following the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-scheut">Battle of Scheut</a>, he led a small band that entered the city by night and drove out the Flemish garrison. He was murdered in 1388 at the instigation of the lord of Gaasbeek, and a bronze memorial near the Grand-Place of Brussels honours him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/everard-tserclaes.jpg" alt="Everard t'Serclaes" width="320" height="207" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sculptor: Juliaan Dillen (1849-1904); Photo made by Viktor Hauk · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brussel_Everard_Serclaes.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Little is known of his early life. He was the son of Everard t'Serclaes and Avesoete vander Noot, and had two brothers, Jean and Niklaas, and a sister, Geertrui. He seems to have followed a military career from his youth: in 1351, still a squire, he is recorded serving King John II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-years-war">Hundred Years' War</a>, for which he received an annual pension of ₶100.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After the death of John III of Brabant on 5 December 1355, his daughter Joanna and her husband Wenceslaus succeeded to the duchy, but Louis II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> disputed their succession, invaded Brabant and seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a> in August 1356. T'Serclaes went into exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maastricht">Maastricht</a>. On the night of 24 October 1356 he came back through the Sonian Forest, scaled the city walls with his supporters and expelled the Flemish garrison, which allowed Joanna and Wenceslaus to make their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joyous-entry-of-1356">Joyous Entry</a> into the city.</p><p>He entered ducal service the same year and is first named as a knight in 1358. From the early 1370s he was active in the politics of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a>, serving as schepen in 1372, 1377, 1382 and 1387, four times in fifteen years and against the rules. In 1374 he was one of nine representatives of Brussels in the negotiations between the ducal authorities and the Brabantian cities after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-baesweiler">Battle of Baesweiler</a>. In 1381 he bought the castle and lordship of Kruikenburg from the Lord of Wezemaal.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Joanna's long conflict with William I of Guelders and Jülich left her short of money, and Sweder of Abcoude, Lord of Gaasbeek, proposed to acquire the Mayoralty of Rode. The schepen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a> under t'Serclaes blocked the plan. On 26 March 1388 men acting for Abcoude ambushed him near Lennik and badly mutilated him. He was brought to Brussels, received the sacraments and died on 31 March, and was buried in St Gertrude's Church in Ternat. The killing caused unrest in Brussels, whose forces destroyed Gaasbeek Castle, while Abcoude fled to Diest. His eldest son, also Everard, became Lord of Kruikenburg and was beheaded during riots in Brussels in 1421.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Everard t'Serclaes” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Duke John III in 1355 without a male heir provoked the War of the Brabantine Succession: Louis of Male, count of Flanders, defeated the Brabanters at the Battle of Scheut in August 1356 and took Brussels, until a small band under <b>Everard t'Serclaes</b> recaptured the city that October.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-brabant">Duchy of Brabant · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in The Star, in what is now <a href="/countries/belgium">Belgium</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.85,4.35&amp;year=1364" aria-label="The Star on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Everard t'Serclaes"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5235 5772h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5235 5772h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=50.85,4.35&amp;year=1364">What was here in 1364? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
